Patric Mueller wrote:
 > Ray Dillinger wrote:

[...]

 > I don't think that this group features a
 > representative player set.

Probably no self-selected subset of a large set can
be "representative", self-selection is already a
distinction from the rest. This is, however, very
likely the best one can do.

 > NetHack gets downloaded 13'000 times a month from
 > sourceforge alone, this group has 1588 members and
 > 101 recent authors (determined by google groups).

The "recent authors" count may well be correct, but
the members count is the number of people
*subscribing to Google Groups* who *also subscribe to
rgrn*, a tiny fraction of all rgrn readers, and
utterly ignores the others seeing this newsgroup
through other interfaces. It would amaze me if there
were fewer than a couple of hundred thousand lurkers
to this newsgroup, based on newsgroup readership
counts and poster to lurker counts back when
Arbitron was still capable of counting readers for
newsgroups, and based on a huge growth in Usenet
participation since then.

         A smaller fraction of Net participants use
         Usenet than back in the days when Usenet and
         email was all the Net provided (the best
         estimate I've seen was that 18% of all Net
         users use Usenet), but the growth in Net
         users since then has been astonishing; China
         alone has more Net users than the US has
         adult citizens.

I may be wrong on that guess, though. What was once
"Usenet" with a few dozen newsgroups is now
"Usenet-like" with, last time I saw, 120,000
"newsgroups". That splits the audience pretty
dramatically. In all that clutter, even finding any
one newsgroup is challenging.

Still, with over 100,000 downloads per year, and the
game around for 20 years, I don't feel too bad about
guessing that two years worth of NetHack downloaders
are also rgrn readers.

 > But besides the IRC channel I don't know a place
 > with a NetHack community big enough.

Wouldn't you expect almost all of them to _also_ be
reading here? IRC is a dreadful communications
mechanism, IMO.

 >> Alternatively, I could make a completely
